UID:sat-research-creation-residency-arts-technologies-2026-28@artificialnou
 veau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T210036Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261018
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:Deadline: SAT: Research-Creation Residency Program in Arts and Tech
 nologies 2026-28 (Montreal)
DESCRIPTION:The Societe des arts technologiques (SAT) in Montreal runs a re
 search-creation residency program in arts and technologies for artists and
  collectives working in the performing arts and/or media arts. The residen
 cy funds a research-creation stage of a project in technological arts rath
 er than the finalisation of a finished work\, so the call weighs the explo
 ration process and the innovation in it above a polished outcome. Each sel
 ected project receives a CAD 10\,000 grant\, whether the applicant is an i
 ndividual artist or a collective\, plus access to SAT spaces and equipment
 \, technical and creative support from the SAT teams\, mentoring and train
 ing activities\, and documentation and archiving. Access to spaces and equ
 ipment is assessed against the project's needs and granted subject to avai
 lability. Travel\, meal and accommodation costs beyond the grant are not c
 overed. Open to professional artists and artistic collectives worldwide\, 
 at any level of experience and from any primary discipline\, provided the 
 profile and project suit immersive technologies\; collectives must submit 
 a joint portfolio showing they can carry out the project independently\, p
 articularly the visual and sound creation inside a dome. Each application 
 must include a form and supporting documents setting out the concept\, the
  technical solutions considered\, and what will be explored during the res
 idency. One application per project and per person\; incomplete applicatio
 ns are not evaluated\; use permanent\, downloadable links rather than WeTr
 ansfer or similar temporary services. Accommodations and alternative suppo
 rt documents (large print and so on) are available on request at residence
 s@sat.qc.ca. Deadline 18 October 2026. Apply through the Google Form linke
 d from the SAT open-call page.\n\nOrganisation: Societe des arts technolog
 iques (SAT)\nLocation: Societe des arts technologiques\, 1201 Boulevard Sa
 int-Laurent\, Montreal\, QC\, Canada. Open to professional artists and col
 lectives from anywhere in the world\; no nationality restriction stated.\n
 Award: CAD 10\,000 grant per selected project\, for individual artists and
  collectives alike. Also included: access to SAT workspaces during the res
 idency periods\, access to equipment and software resources subject to ava
 ilability\, mentoring and training activities (on site and remote)\, creat
 ive and technical support from the SAT teams\, and documentation and archi
 ving of the work. Travel\, meals and accommodation beyond the grant are NO
 T covered. No application fee.\nDuration: Two months\, extendable up to th
 ree months depending on the project\; residencies run across the 2026-28 p
 rogram.\n\nMore: https://sat.qc.ca/en/calls/research-creation-residency-pr
 ogram-in-arts-technologies-2026-28?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://sat.qc.ca/en/calls/research-creation-residency-program-in-arts-
 technologies-2026-28?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refe
 rral
LOCATION:Societe des arts technologiques\, 1201 Boulevard Saint-Laurent\, M
 ontreal\, QC\, Canada. Open to professional artists and collectives from a
 nywhere in the world\; no nationality restriction stated.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:guggenheim-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T210036Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Guggenheim Fellowship 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Guggenheim Fellowship is awarded to mid-career individuals 
 who have already demonstrated exceptional capacity for productive scholars
 hip or exceptional creative ability in the arts\, giving them six to twelv
 e months of funded time to work with as much creative freedom as possible.
  Around 175 to 200 fellows are chosen each year from a field of several th
 ousand applicants (a success rate of roughly five percent)\, across more t
 han 50 fields spanning the creative arts\, the humanities\, the natural sc
 iences and the social sciences\, including architecture\, film\, music\, p
 hotography\, fine arts\, computer science and interdisciplinary work. ELIG
 IBILITY: citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada\
 ; applicants must be mid-career with a significant record of published or 
 exhibited work\, and current students and previous Guggenheim Fellows are 
 not eligible. APPLICATION: through the Foundation's own portal\, which ope
 ns in late summer 2026\, so check the site if it is not yet live. The appl
 ication deadline is 15 September 2026 with no late submissions accepted\; 
 work samples are due 29 September 2026 and reference letters 13 October 20
 26. Results are announced in late April 2027. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: Joh
 n Simon Guggenheim Memorial Foundation\nLocation: Anywhere\; the fellowshi
 p funds the fellow's own work rather than residence at a host institution.
  Open to citizens and permanent residents of the United States and Canada.
 \nAward: Variable cash grant sized to the applicant's proposed budget and 
 needs\; the Foundation does not publish a fixed figure or range\, and rece
 nt awards have typically run into the tens of thousands of US dollars. Rou
 ghly 175 to 200 fellowships are awarded each year across more than 50 fiel
 ds. The Foundation charges no application fee and has no affiliated applic
 ation consultants.\nDuration: Six to twelve months of supported work\n\nMo
 re: https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.gf.org/program/how-to-apply/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Anywhere\; the fellowship funds the fellow's own work rather than 
 residence at a host institution. Open to citizens and permanent residents 
 of the United States and Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:video-pool-matr-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T210036Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Video Pool: Media Art + Technology Residency (MATR) 2027
DESCRIPTION:Video Pool's Media Art + Technology Residency supports experime
 ntal\, independent\, technology-based work. The selected artist or artists
  develop a new project (or adapt and transform an existing one) in direct 
 response to the intimate scale and specific character of the Poolside Gall
 ery\, culminating in an exhibition there. The call is deliberately open on
  medium: video\, sound\, installation\, digital media\, performance\, inte
 ractive technologies\, expanded cinema and interdisciplinary practices are
  all welcome. VP asks proposals to speak clearly to your practice\, the qu
 estions\, materials or ideas that drive the work\, and how that work might
  grow\, shift or take new form inside the gallery. It is explicitly intere
 sted in expansive proposals\, work that pushes past the gallery's four wal
 ls to engage its surrounding spaces (hallways\, windows\, projection wall)
  or that reimagines what the gallery itself can hold\, and in projects tha
 t engage technology as a creative\, critical\, aesthetic or socio-politica
 l tool. Priority is given to artists from equity-seeking communities whose
  practices contribute to the advancement of experimental media arts. Resid
 ents get full access to VP's available equipment\, facilities and technica
 l resources throughout the residency\, and applicants are encouraged to re
 view the equipment and facilities list before proposing. ELIGIBILITY: open
  to VP members (this is a member submission call)\; no nationality restric
 tion is stated\, but the residency and exhibition are in person in Winnipe
 g. APPLICATION: artist bio (1\,500 characters)\, headshot\, description of
  your artistic practice (3\,000 characters)\, project proposal for MATR (3
 \,000 characters)\, equipment and facility needs (750 characters)\, descri
 ption of how you would use the space (750 characters)\, CV\, up to five im
 ages (optional) and up to two video or documentation links. Submit via the
  online form at videopool.org/matr-2027-application/ by 30 August 2026\; a
 lternative submission formats can be arranged by contacting Emma (emma@vid
 eopool.org) or Iyunade (iyunade@videopool.org). General enquiries: info@vi
 deopool.org\, 204-949-9134.\n\nOrganisation: Video Pool Media Arts Centre\
 nLocation: In person at Video Pool Media Arts Centre and its Poolside Gall
 ery\, 221-100 Arthur Street\, Winnipeg\, Manitoba R3B 1H3\, Canada.\nAward
 : CAD 2\,250 artist fee\, covering both the residency period and the subse
 quent Poolside Gallery exhibition. Residents also get full access to VP's 
 equipment\, facilities and technical resources\; the Poolside Gallery itse
 lf is equipped with two projectors and four speakers. Applicants must be V
 P members\, so a membership is purchased separately\, but there is no sepa
 rate application fee.\nDuration: Up to two months to develop and install t
 he project\, including one month of dedicated access to the Poolside Galle
 ry. Residency takes place in Winter 2027\; exhibition dates TBD.\n\nMore: 
 https://videopool.org/media-arts-technology-residency-2027-call-for-applic
 ations/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://videopool.org/media-arts-technology-residency-2027-call-for-app
 lications/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person at Video Pool Media Arts Centre and its Poolside Gallery
 \, 221-100 Arthur Street\, Winnipeg\, Manitoba R3B 1H3\, Canada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:toronto-arts-council-artists-in-the-library-2027@artificialnouveau.gith
 ub.io
DTSTAMP:20260902T210036Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261006
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261007
SUMMARY:Deadline: Artists in the Library 2027 (Toronto Arts Council)
DESCRIPTION:Created in 2014 as a partnership between Toronto Arts Council a
 nd Toronto Public Library\, Artists in the Library gives Toronto artists p
 aid opportunities and space while widening access to the arts in Etobicoke
 \, York\, North York\, East York and Scarborough. It funds professional ar
 tists\, arts organisations and collectives in any artistic discipline to c
 reate and deliver free community-engaged arts programming at a Toronto Pub
 lic Library branch. TAC encourages applications from its equity priority g
 roups and from artists working in their local communities. WHAT RECIPIENTS
  GET OUT OF IT: a chance to explore their practice in a community setting\
 , access to branch space and facilities\, meaningful engagement with the b
 ranch and its community\, close support from the branch team\, experience 
 navigating a multi-stakeholder environment\, and the delivery of a communi
 ty-engaged arts project from start to finish. WHAT COUNTS: community engag
 ement is required but its format can vary\, covering exhibitions\, install
 ations\, performances\, concerts\, screenings\, readings\, filmmaking\, st
 orytelling\, workshops\, artist talks\, participatory programmes\, arts ed
 ucation and mentorship. Permanent exhibitions and installations are not el
 igible. Projects are produced by the recipient\, hosted and supported by t
 he assigned branch\, and must be delivered on site within branch operating
  hours. SCALE: because host slots at TPL branches are limited\, this is no
 t for one-off workshops or small-scale events\; the offering must be subst
 antial with a budget to match. LOCATION: there is no list of eligible bran
 ches for 2026. Applicants indicate a preferred area or neighbourhood outsi
 de the downtown core and TPL staff assign the branch based on suitability 
 and availability\, so a different branch than the one requested may be ass
 igned. For Toronto and East York\, downtown is excluded\, defined as south
  of St Clair\, east of Jane and west of the Don Valley. Only one branch ma
 y be proposed. ELIGIBILITY: individuals must be Canadian citizens\, perman
 ent residents\, have a pending PR application\, or be a protected person\,
  must hold a Social Insurance Number\, and must have lived in the City of 
 Toronto for at least one year before the deadline and live and work there 
 at least eight months a year (no PO boxes). Artists in graduate study may 
 not apply for work related to their programme without a letter from the pr
 ogramme director. Organisations must be incorporated non-profit arts organ
 isations\, and collectives must operate on a non-profit basis\, be located
  in Toronto\, and have both members (for a pair) or a majority of members 
 resident in Toronto\; successful collectives must supply bank account deta
 ils in the collective's name. IMPORTANT: proposals must align with TPL's p
 rogramming policy\, and proposals that TPL staff judge unsuitable for a br
 anch or impossible in its context will not be funded\, so read the 'Workin
 g With Toronto Public Library' section of the guidelines. First-time appli
 cants are encouraged to consult the programme manager and to attend the Zo
 om office hours on eligibility and project fit (17 September) and on budge
 ts (29 September). Contact: Lena Golubtsova\, lena@torontoartscouncil.org.
  Deadline 6 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Toronto Arts Council\, in partn
 ership with Toronto Public Library\nLocation: Toronto\, Canada. Programmin
 g takes place at a Toronto Public Library branch outside the downtown core
 \, in Etobicoke York\, North York\, Toronto and East York\, or Scarborough
 . Applicants must be City of Toronto residents.\nAward: Maximum grant of C
 AD 20\,000\, which may cover up to 100 percent of project costs. Payment o
 f professional artist fees is a requirement of the programme. Recipients a
 lso get access to space and facilities at the assigned library branch for 
 arts programming and presentation\, and support from the branch team. Fund
 ed by the City of Toronto. No application fee is mentioned.\nDuration: Pro
 gramming must take place between September and December 2027\n\nMore: http
 s://torontoartscouncil.org/grants/artists-in-the-library/?utm_source=artif
 icialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://torontoartscouncil.org/grants/artists-in-the-library/?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Toronto\, Canada. Programming takes place at a Toronto Public Libr
 ary branch outside the downtown core\, in Etobicoke York\, North York\, To
 ronto and East York\, or Scarborough. Applicants must be City of Toronto r
 esidents.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
